#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065 :topic/id #uuid "d7019ce6-160a-5aa4-9e08-78782f485dfa" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054425 :question/id #uuid "16e0f524-49b5-549d-8e60-8597379c4350" :question/math-academy-id "e-13134" :question/problem "List the elements of the following set: \n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{a, c,3, \\{c,3 \\}, \\emptyset, \\{\\emptyset\\}\\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The elements of the set $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\na, \\quad c, \\quad 3, \\quad \\{c,3\\}, \\quad \\emptyset, \\quad \\{\\emptyset\\}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!**\n\n- The elements $c,3,$ and $\\{c,3\\}$ are three separate entities. The first two are the elements $c$ and $3,$ and the third is a set *containing* these two elements.\n\n- The elements $\\emptyset$ and $\\{ \\emptyset \\}$ are also separate entities. The first one is the empty set (i.e., the set containing no elements), while the second is a set that contains the empty set."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "33551349-bb39-5a79-bf56-a4df753201ed" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Elements of a Set"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054426 :question/id #uuid "046f7284-3715-555c-bdc9-c108db061205" :question/math-academy-id "e-13135" :question/problem "Which of the following is **not** a set?\n\nI. $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$\n\nII. $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$\n\nIII. $2, 4, 6$\n\nIV. $\\emptyset$" :question/worked-solution "A set is a collection of elements listed between two braces (curly brackets). However, note that the null set (the set that does not contain any elements) can be written as either $\\{\\}$ or $\\emptyset.$\n\n- $\\{ 2, 4, 6 \\}$ is a set. The elements are $2,$ $4,$ and $6,$ and they are listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\{ \\{ 2 \\}, \\{ 4, 6 \\} \\}$ is a set. The elements are $\\{ 2 \\}$ and $\\{ 4, 6 \\},$ and are listed between the two outermost braces.\n\n- $2, 4, 6$ is *not* a set. The numbers $2,$ $4,$ and $6$ are *not* listed between two braces.\n\n- $\\emptyset$ is the empty set. Therefore, it is a set.\n\nIn conclusion, only III is not a set."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de59db4d-82d5-5bad-a559-bf5396375355" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054427 :question/id #uuid "5404d545-570b-53d7-b16a-b23a371d4abd" :question/math-academy-id "e-13136" :question/problem "Write the following statement using element notation:\n\n$\\qquad$ $1$ and $2$ are elements of a set $S,$ but $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are not." :question/worked-solution "If $a$ is an element of $S,$ we write $a \\in S.$ Otherwise, if $a$ is *not* an element of $S,$ we write $a \\notin S.$\n\n- Since $1$ and $2$ are elements of $S,$ we write $1,2 \\in S.$\n\n- Since $3,$ $4,$ and $5$ are *not* elements of $S,$ we write $3,4,5 \\notin S.$\n\nAll together, we have the following statement:\n\n$$\n1,2 \\in S\\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad 3,4,5 \\notin S\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900f0d3d-6a5c-53b2-b987-eee1a8963b6b" :knowledge-point/title "Using Element Notation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054428 :question/id #uuid "0684e366-bcf4-5b08-8eae-6600a88e2920" :question/math-academy-id "e-13137" :question/problem "Express the following sets using an ellipsis:\n\n- The set $X$ of all positive integers greater than or equal to $10$\n\n- The set $Y$ of integers less than zero\n\n- The set $A$ of all natural numbers greater than $5$ and less than $5\\, 005$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an ellipsis means \"and so on up to\" if it's followed by a specific element, or \"and so on\" if not.\n\n- To express the set $X,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than or equal to $10,$ which is $10.$ The next integer is $11,$ the one after that is $12,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nX = \\{10, 11, 12, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\n- To express the set $Y,$ we start with the first integer less than zero, which is $-1.$ The next integer less than zero is $-2,$ the one after that is $-3,$ and so on, which we denote with an ellipsis:\n\n$$\nY = \\{ -1, -2, -3, \\ldots \\}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that it is conventional to write a set of numbers from least to greatest, just like is shown on the number line. Writing the elements of $Y$ from least to greatest, we have\n\n$$\nY = \\{ \\ldots , -3, -2, -1\\}.\n$$\n\n- To express the set $A,$ we start with the first positive integer greater than $5,$ which is $6.$ The next integer is $7,$ the one after that is $8,$ and so on up to $5\\, 004$ (the largest integer less than $5\\, 005$). So, we have\n\n$$\nA = \\{6, 7, 8, \\ldots , 5004\\}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ceb6c84-a16b-5519-abd6-d2bb72ea81d3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Set Corresponding to a Description"}] :topic/math-academy-id 45 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :topic/title "Sets"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049565 :topic/id #uuid "00161849-870c-5cf5-81ba-5ddd9fe08fc3" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083162 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082979 :question/id #uuid "6353522b-b5c4-545d-8ef5-0f26c10e45aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3949" :question/problem "What are the real and imaginary parts of the complex number $5 - 2\\textrm{i}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a given complex number\n\n$$\nz = a + b \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n- the number $a$ is called the **real part** of $z$, and\n- the number $b$ is called the **imaginary part** of $z$.\n\nTherefore, the real part of the given complex number is $5$, and the imaginary part is $-2$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf530e0-ce1c-5b7f-a6ca-802f369247f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082980 :question/id #uuid "5eb7d954-4df5-5cec-91b3-dd21773064b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3953" :question/problem "What is the real part of the complex number $-\\sqrt 7 \\,\\textrm{i}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a given complex number\n\n$$\nz = a + b \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n- the number $a$ is called the **real part** of $z$, and\n- the number $b$ is called the **imaginary part** of $z$.\n\nWe can think of the number $-\\sqrt 7 \\,\\textrm{i}$ as having a real part of zero:\n\n$$\n-\\sqrt 7\\,\\textrm{i} = 0 - \\sqrt 7\\,\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the real part of the given complex number is $0$ and the imaginary part is $-\\sqrt 7$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d88dd57-9a54-50ab-ac39-23d3103ac017" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Real Part of a Purely Imaginary Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082981 :question/id #uuid "89c2a344-1b3a-5b8d-b600-3128255cf040" :question/math-academy-id "e-3954" :question/problem "What is the imaginary part of the complex number $-6$?" :question/worked-solution "For a given complex number\n\n$$\nz = a + b \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n- the number $a$ is called the **real part** of $z$, and\n- the number $b$ is called the **imaginary part** of $z$.\n\nWe can think of the number $-6$ as having an imaginary part of zero:\n\n$$\n-6 = -6+ 0\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the complex number $-6$, the imaginary part is $0$ and the real part is $-6$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d7d8bfa-7372-5bd4-a317-4734fd96c931" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Imaginary Part of a Real Number"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083165 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082982 :question/id #uuid "240d2dc0-badd-531c-8097-a99eec6b69da" :question/math-academy-id "e-3955" :question/problem "What is the real part of the complex number $\\sqrt 3+6\\textrm{i}-2$?" :question/worked-solution "For a given complex number\n\n$$\nz = a + b \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\n- the number $a$ is called the **real part** of $z$, and\n- the number $b$ is called the **imaginary part** of $z$.\n\nRewriting the given complex number in the form $a + b \\textrm{i}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 3+6\\textrm{i}-2=\\left(\\sqrt 3-2\\right)+6\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the real part of the given complex number is $\\sqrt 3-2$ and the imaginary part is $6$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc460e40-11b1-5597-a512-2ba1df00aa51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Real and Imaginary Parts of a Complex Number Written in Nonstandard Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 735 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050191}] :topic/title "Complex Numbers"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300 :topic/id #uuid "3dfc702e-510d-534f-98bc-06c367122acf" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133745 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133555 :question/id #uuid "1e830784-49d8-5cc7-9703-d1a7513333e9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4861" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are irrational?\n\n1. $\\sqrt 3$\n2. $\\dfrac32$\n3. $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2} 2$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following:\n\n- A rational number is any number that can be written as the ratio of two integers.\n- Any real number is irrational if it is not rational.\n\nNow, let's go through each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt 3$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n- $\\dfrac32$ is not an irrational number. It is a rational number.\n- $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt2}2$ is an irrational number. It cannot be expressed as the ratio of two integers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0379ee2-f2ed-5a3f-b23d-b7175298b333" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Irrational and Real Numbers"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133859 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133672 :question/id #uuid "e1b87de2-abb4-57ec-8928-674dfa474935" :question/math-academy-id "e-8176" :question/problem "![](images/12/12f69e1bc4941538759b236578e6679b353b714974321eb4d9c7acbd14dc28ab.png)\n\nIn which section should the number $-\\sqrt{15}$ be placed in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each category from top to bottom:\n\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is a real number $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not a rational number $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not an integer $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n- $-\\sqrt{15}$ is not a natural number $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct section of the diagram is IV (the real numbers)."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e108a956-ba00-5854-beaf-7d85d47d1397" :knowledge-point/title "Placing a Real Number on a Diagram"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133717 :question/id #uuid "13b7c475-3f64-5cc0-9535-2aba67abe708" :question/math-academy-id "e-9591" :question/problem "Which of the following is an irrational number?\n\n$4, \\qquad 3.1415926 \\ldots, \\qquad \\dfrac{5}{13}, \\qquad 1.27, \\qquad 5.7232323 \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "An irrational number is a number that cannot be written as the ratio of two integers.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of our numbers.\n\n- $4$ is a *rational* number. It can be written as $\\dfrac{4}{1}$.\n- $3.1415926 \\ldots$ is an irrational number. The digits after the decimal point in $3.1415926 \\ldots$ extend forever, but there is no regular pattern.\n- $\\dfrac{5}{13}$ is a *rational* number.\n- $1.27$ is a *rational* number since it is a terminating decimal.\n- $5.7232323\\ldots$ extends forever but has a repeating pattern ($23$ repeats forever). Therefore, it's a *rational* number.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only $3.1415926$ is an irrational number."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30895d97-eca9-5bc7-92f4-b9e4c6c3be11" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decimals as Irrational"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2205 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049796}] :topic/title "The Real Number System"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404 :topic/id #uuid "46172e8e-6e68-554f-baaa-252a31973372" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133501 :question/id #uuid "127ac025-aa32-505a-98d8-27721a37fdbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-12829" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\sqrt{126}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" :question/worked-solution "Using a calculator, we find that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126}=11.224\\,972\\,160\\,...\n$$\n\nNow, we round to $3$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{126} \\approx 11.225\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2c606764-287a-500f-aa91-e6a68bec8860" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Decimal Approximation of a Square Root Surd"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133500 :question/id #uuid "0b823033-1f76-5656-a284-b94f33f3e359" :question/math-academy-id "e-12828" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2},\\qquad \\sqrt{8},\\qquad \\sqrt{16}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{2}=1.414\\,213\\,562\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{8}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{8}=2.828\\,427\\,124\\,...$\n\n- $\\sqrt{16}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $16$ is a perfect square, we can get rid of the square root symbol ($\\sqrt{\\phantom{x}}$) and obtain a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16}=\\sqrt{4^2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{8}$ only."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf6ecc42-d84a-584f-9ccc-d6a987c89394" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Integers"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133724 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133515 :question/id #uuid "12b5f86f-9e86-5c1b-8773-38e2b69a6417" :question/math-academy-id "e-13357" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}, \\qquad \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}} = 0.645\\,497\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{64}{81}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $64$ and $81$ are perfect squares, we can get rid of the square root symbol $(\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\cdot 8}{9\\cdot 9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}} = 1.195\\,228\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{12}}$ and $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{10}{7}}$ only."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1e6bea-5399-5dba-b2b1-f59441915324" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Surds: Square Roots of Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133725 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133502 :question/id #uuid "9e145904-a9bf-59fc-9070-339b0e133de9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12830" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are surds?\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{2},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}},\\qquad \\sqrt[3]{11}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A surd is a number containing a radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$ that cannot be written as a positive or negative whole number or fraction.\n\nSurds can be square roots $\\sqrt{\\phantom{0}}$, cube roots $\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}},$ or higher roots such as $\\sqrt[4]{\\phantom{0}}, \\sqrt[5]{\\phantom{0}},\\ldots,$ etc.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each number in turn.\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{2} = 1.259\\,921\\,\\ldots$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{\\dfrac{1}{27}}$ is **NOT** a surd. Since $1$ and $27$ are perfect cubes, we can get rid of the cube root symbol $(\\sqrt[3]{\\phantom{0}})$ and obtain a regular fraction.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{27}} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{3}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ is a surd. Using a calculator, we find that $\\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.223\\,980\\,\\ldots$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is: $\\sqrt[3]{2}$ and $\\sqrt[3]{11}$ only."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "115ee606-48f2-5a63-a5c6-fd8e3e1dbec0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cube Root Surds"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2204 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050315}] :topic/title "Surds"}]}
